<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Gunnery Control Test Lab - Fire-Control Observability Logger
  Developer-only.  Belongs in testlab, NOT in the shipped mod.

  PURPOSE
  docs/FIRE_CONTROL_BEHAVIOR.md carries rows marked UNTESTED and UNKNOWN,
  mostly the "Prefer My Target ON" column.  They are unresolved because there
  was no way to see, after the fact, what the turrets were doing at an
  interesting moment.  This script samples turret fire-control state during
  ordinary free play so a capture can be read back and a row promoted to LIVE.

  WHY THIS LIVES IN MD AND NOT IN LUA
  docs/HANDOFF-fire-control-observability.md section 3 proposed two cooperating
  loggers, a Lua state sampler plus an MD firing-solution prober.  MD turned out
  to read almost the entire field list natively, so the Lua half collapsed away:

    player.age                          timestamp, the only MD clock
    player.ship.pilot.command           matches Lua aicommandraw exactly
    player.target                       props-9.00/.../scriptproperties.xml:2438
    player.ship.turrets.operational.list             scriptproperties.xml:539
    $turret.mode                                     scriptproperties.xml:1448
    $target.lastattacker / .lastattacktime      scriptproperties.xml:187-188
    $turret.rotation.yaw / .pitch                 scriptproperties.xml:58-60

  $turret.mode is strictly better than the Lua group-level read the handoff
  assumed: it resolves holdfire and autoassist per turret, which
  GetTurretGroupMode2 does not distinguish.  $target.lastattacker likewise
  supersedes GetLastAttackInfo and needs no ffi, which matters because
  testlab/ui/testlab.lua deliberately contains zero require("ffi") and zero C.
  calls.  Keeping it that way is why only two fields are pushed in from Lua.

  Running in MD also means the sampler ticks with every menu closed, which is
  the entire free-play use case.  A Lua timer's ability to fire frameless was
  never verified; here it is a nice-to-have rather than load-bearing.

  TRANSPORT
  Same flat-scalar contract as the scenario script: the engine prepends '$' to
  every Lua string key (research KB, "Lua->MD AddUITriggeredEvent transport
  contract", live-tested 2026-08-04).  Component ids arrive as components
  because Lua converts them with ConvertStringToLuaID.

    observe_toggle {enabled = true|false}
    observe_mark   {}
    observe_state  {softtgt =, aimtgt =, prefer = true|false}

  observe_state is only sent while a Direct-control session exists.  In plain
  free play it never arrives and the log simply carries player.target, which is
  correct rather than degraded.

  TWO OPEN QUESTIONS THIS SCRIPT ANSWERS FROM ITS OWN OUTPUT
  1. excludeself semantics.  common.xsd:21722 declares default="false" while its
     own documentation string says "True by default" - a contradiction in the
     shipped schema that no live test has settled.  Rather than guess, every
     turret is raycast TWICE, logged as los_ex and los_inc.  One capture
     containing a turret shooting through open space and a turret masked by the
     player's own hull settles it with no throwaway probe cue.
  2. Live barrel or mounted base.  scriptproperties.xml:58 documents rotation as
     "relative to parent", which for a turret is traverse angle relative to the
     hull, exactly the wanted quantity.  componentslot has a .rotation versus
     .staticrotation split where staticrotation is annotated "ignoring offset
     changes due to animation" (scriptproperties.xml:1483-1486), implying plain
     .rotation does include animation, but that is inference.  Logging yaw and
     pitch every sample answers it directly: swing the ship hard and read
     whether the numbers move.

     yaw and pitch are logged rather than rotation.forward.x/y/z because they
     are documented angle members certain to exist, and an unparseable property
     expression kills an MD cue silently.  The full geometric cone test is a
     later refinement, only if the barrel proves live AND los plus range turns
     out to be insufficient.

  VOCABULARY
  Deliberately NOT classified here.  los_ex, los_inc and inrange are logged as
  raw booleans and the analyst maps them to ON SOLUTION / OUT OF RANGE / MASKED
  / OUT OF ARC.  Pre-labelling a line MASKED before question 1 is settled would
  bake in a guess.  check_line_of_sight is fine as an API name; the condition
  names are the four above and nothing else.

  RELOAD NOTE: any change to THIS file requires a full game restart, because
  testlab/ md is only reloadable together with the rest of the extension.
  See docs/RELOADING.md.
-->
<mdscript name="X4GunneryTestLabObserve" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="../../../../#modding/xsd/md.xsd">
  <cues>
    <cue name="ObserveRoot">
      <actions>
        <!-- Off by default.  A long play session must not flood debug.log
             because the extension happens to be installed; nothing is written
             until the owner arms it from the Test Lab menu. -->
        <set_value name="$Enabled" exact="false"/>
        <set_value name="$MarkCount" exact="0"/>
        <set_value name="$Prefer" exact="false"/>
        <!-- Seeded so the ObserveLoop time guard has something to compare
             against on its very first check.  Derived from player.age rather
             than written as a bare -1 so it carries the time datatype: player.age
             is a time (suffix s, scriptproperties.xml:2396) and comparing a time
             against a plain number is a type error, not a false. -->
        <set_value name="$LastSample" exact="player.age - 1s"/>
      </actions>
      <cues>

        <!-- ObserveToggle
             AddUITriggeredEvent("X4GunneryTestLabObserve", "observe_toggle",
               {enabled = true|false})
             The master switch.  Resetting ObserveLoop is what actually starts
             sampling: the loop parks on an unsatisfied condition while
             disabled, so it needs a kick to re-evaluate. -->
        <cue name="ObserveToggle" instantiate="true">
          <conditions>
            <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4GunneryTestLabObserve'" control="'observe_toggle'"/>
          </conditions>
          <actions>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$Enabled" exact="@event.param3.$enabled == true"/>
            <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST OBSERVE] enabled=' + ObserveRoot.$Enabled + ' t=' + player.age"/>
            <!-- No reset_cue here.  ObserveLoop instantiates on its own
                 checkinterval, so arming is picked up within a second by the
                 engine's own polling, and poking a cue that instantiates is
                 what produced the re-entrant freeze in the first place. -->
          </actions>
        </cue>

        <!-- ObserveMark
             AddUITriggeredEvent("X4GunneryTestLabObserve", "observe_mark")
             Stamps the interesting instant so the window is greppable
             afterwards.  The label is generated here as an incrementing
             counter rather than typed by the owner: mid-combat the whole point
             is one click, and "which mark was that" is answered by reading the
             surrounding lines.  Marks are accepted even while logging is off,
             so a mark is never silently swallowed. -->
        <cue name="ObserveMark" instantiate="true">
          <conditions>
            <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4GunneryTestLabObserve'" control="'observe_mark'"/>
          </conditions>
          <actions>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$MarkCount" exact="ObserveRoot.$MarkCount + 1"/>
            <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST MARK] label=mark_' + ObserveRoot.$MarkCount + ' enabled=' + ObserveRoot.$Enabled + ' t=' + player.age"/>
          </actions>
        </cue>

        <!-- ObserveState
             AddUITriggeredEvent("X4GunneryTestLabObserve", "observe_state",
               {softtgt =, aimtgt =, prefer = true|false})
             The only two facts MD cannot see for itself: the engine SOFT target
             (MD has player.target and no soft equivalent - scriptproperties.xml
             has no player.softtarget at all) and the mod session's
             preferAllTurrets / aimTargetID.  Pushed from Lua only while a
             Direct-control session exists. -->
        <cue name="ObserveState" instantiate="true">
          <conditions>
            <event_ui_triggered screen="'X4GunneryTestLabObserve'" control="'observe_state'"/>
          </conditions>
          <actions>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget" exact="@event.param3.$softtgt"/>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$AimTarget" exact="@event.param3.$aimtgt"/>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$Prefer" exact="@event.param3.$prefer == true"/>
          </actions>
        </cue>

        <!-- ObserveLoop
             The sampler.  checkinterval paces it AND re-polls the gate: a
             condition-only cue with no checkinterval is not re-evaluated on its
             own, so an $Enabled that flips to true after this cue first failed
             its condition could otherwise never be noticed.

             instantiate plus checkinterval, NOT reset_cue cue="this".  The
             first version of this cue combined checkinterval with a trailing
             self-reset, which froze the game hard and wrote a 4.3 GB debug.log
             in seconds: the reset re-satisfied the condition immediately, so
             the cue re-fired without any game time passing (every line in that
             log carried the identical timestamp 247034.45).  instantiate lets
             the engine own the pacing, which is the shipped idiom - see
             StationPeaceful_Zoom_VisibilityCheck,
             .x4-research-cache/extracted/scripts-9.00/md/cinematiccamera.xml:2004,
             which is checkinterval="1s" instantiate="true" with no self-reset.

             $LastSample is a belt-and-braces guard on top of that: no sample is
             ever emitted twice for the same value of player.age, so even if a
             future edit reintroduces a re-entrant path it cannot run away
             again.  A freeze that needs the process killed is expensive enough
             to be worth two lines of paranoia.

             1s is a deliberate compromise: fast enough that a turret going
             silent is visible, slow enough that the per-turret raycast sweep
             stays affordable.  Vanilla's own solution check breaks on the first
             hit, so an all-turrets sweep is strictly more expensive than
             anything the engine does (md-ai.md:49) - hence the hard $Enabled
             gate and the idle path when there is no target. -->
        <cue name="ObserveLoop" instantiate="true" checkinterval="1s">
          <conditions>
            <check_value value="ObserveRoot.$Enabled"/>
            <check_value value="player.age gt ObserveRoot.$LastSample"/>
          </conditions>
          <actions>
            <set_value name="ObserveRoot.$LastSample" exact="player.age"/>
            <!-- player.occupiedship is null in a gunnery chair - it means "in
                 the PILOT seat" (scriptproperties.xml:2416), and the whole mod
                 exists for the case where the player is not in it.  player.ship
                 is the enclosing ship context and is the correct read here. -->
            <do_if value="player.ship">
              <set_value name="$Ship" exact="player.ship"/>

              <!-- The soft target is what Direct-control actually aims at, so
                   it wins when present.  Falling back to player.target is what
                   makes plain free play work with no Lua involvement at all. -->
              <set_value name="$Target" exact="null"/>
              <do_if value="ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget? and ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget.exists">
                <set_value name="$Target" exact="ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget"/>
              </do_if>
              <do_elseif value="player.target?">
                <set_value name="$Target" exact="player.target"/>
              </do_elseif>

              <set_value name="$Now" exact="player.age"/>

              <!-- One STATE line per sample.  hardtgt and softtgt are logged
                   separately on purpose: auto-assist follows the locked target
                   while Direct-control aims at the selected one, and the
                   divergence between them is itself a thing to catch. -->
              <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST STATE] t=' + $Now
                + ' ship=' + $Ship
                + ' shipname=' + $Ship.knownname
                + ' pilot=' + (if $Ship.pilot then $Ship.pilot.command.value else 'none')
                + ' hardtgt=' + (if player.target then player.target else 'none')
                + ' softtgt=' + (if ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget? and ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget then ObserveRoot.$SoftTarget else 'none')
                + ' aimtgt=' + (if ObserveRoot.$AimTarget? and ObserveRoot.$AimTarget then ObserveRoot.$AimTarget else 'none')
                + ' prefer=' + ObserveRoot.$Prefer
                + ' tgt=' + (if $Target then $Target else 'none')
                + ' tgtname=' + (if $Target then $Target.knownname else 'none')
                + ' lasthit_by=' + (if $Target and $Target.lastattacker? then $Target.lastattacker else 'none')
                + ' lasthit_t=' + (if $Target then $Target.lastattacktime else -1)
                + ' turrets=' + $Ship.turrets.operational.count"/>

              <!-- No target means there is nothing to compute a solution
                   against, so the sweep is skipped entirely and only the cheap
                   STATE line is written.  This is the idle path during
                   ordinary flight. -->
              <do_if value="$Target">
                <do_for_each name="$Turret" in="$Ship.turrets.operational.list">
                  <!-- Both raycasts, every turret, every sample.  See the
                       excludeself note in the file header: the shipped schema
                       contradicts itself about the default, so the log carries
                       both answers and the capture settles it. -->
                  <check_line_of_sight name="$LosEx" object="$Turret" target="$Target" excludeself="true" useaimtarget="true"/>
                  <check_line_of_sight name="$LosInc" object="$Turret" target="$Target" excludeself="false" useaimtarget="true"/>

                  <!-- The engine's own definition of in-range, verbatim from
                       shipped combat AI: distance plus half the target's size
                       under max fire range (md-ai.md:47-50).  There is no arc
                       term anywhere in vanilla's firing-solution test. -->
                  <set_value name="$Dist" exact="$Turret.distanceto.{$Target}"/>
                  <set_value name="$InRange" exact="$Dist + ($Target.size / 2) lt $Turret.maxfirerange"/>

                  <debug_text text="'[X4GC TEST SOLUTION] t=' + $Now
                    + ' turret=' + $Turret
                    + ' mode=' + $Turret.mode
                    + ' los_ex=' + $LosEx
                    + ' los_inc=' + $LosInc
                    + ' inrange=' + $InRange
                    + ' dist=' + $Dist
                    + ' maxrange=' + $Turret.maxfirerange
                    + ' tgtsize=' + $Target.size
                    + ' yaw=' + $Turret.rotation.yaw
                    + ' pitch=' + $Turret.rotation.pitch"/>
                </do_for_each>
              </do_if>
            </do_if>
            <!-- Deliberately no reset_cue: instantiate plus checkinterval means
                 the engine schedules the next tick.  Self-resetting here is the
                 exact line that froze the game. -->
          </actions>
        </cue>

      </cues>
    </cue>
  </cues>
</mdscript>
